From: Christopher Owen <3221756+christopherowen@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 17:47:30 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] perf(oneshot): chain the one-shot collectives with
 programmatic dependent launch

Upstream base: christopherowen/dgx-spark-networking 1fd6a1fbd8dc4884057046d1ebd93d585c9e4bcf
with the proxy-pinning patch (tree dab8c4577ac1) and the single-kernel
multi-stage collectives (dgx-spark-networking#7, spark-ds41f#989) applied.

Source: Part A of spark-ds41f#982 / dgx-spark-networking#6, re-placed into
#989's multi-stage kernels. Part B of that change (the zero-copy
next_send_buffer(shape, dtype, *, slot=None) API on RoceOneshotAllReduce and
SparknetOneShotAllReduce with its _send_dev slot views and _host_seq
counter, roce_same_ptr, the `if (src != dst)` guard in roce_copy_pack, the
`if (input != send)` guard in roce_stage_gather_pack, and their CPU and GPU
tests) is left out: no vLLM code calls next_send_buffer, so it does nothing
in this deployment.

Rationale:
The four TileLang collective kernels (oneshot_allreduce, oneshot_allgather,
oneshot_reducescatter, oneshot_exchange) neither waited on nor triggered
programmatic dependent launch, so in a captured graph the edges into and out
of every collective stayed full dependencies while the producers (WO-B,
moe_combine, fused_wqa_wkv) and consumers (mhc_post, attention_front,
gather_columns_into) around them are on the PDL chain.

1. T.pdl_sync() (cudaGridDependencySynchronize) is the first statement of
   each kernel, at the top level so every thread waits, before the epoch
   load of counters[0] and before any access to inp, out or the pinned
   region; the generated CUDA has only local declarations ahead of it.
   TileLang launches a kernel with a wait with programmatic stream
   serialization, so the collective's grid can be resident while its
   producer drains.
2. T.pdl_trigger() (launch_dependents) follows the doorbell. The all-reduce
   has one stage and triggers right after it, as #982 did. In the
   multi-stage kernels the trigger sits inside `for s in T.serial(num_stages)`
   after the doorbell block, guarded by `if s == num_stages - 1`: once per
   launch, after the last stage's doorbell and before that stage's
   roce_wait_peers. A single-stage launch therefore runs exactly #982's
   measured pdl_post_doorbell placement; every captured (decode) launch is
   single-stage, because the runtime pipelines only outside capture.
   Triggering after stage 0 instead would make dependents' CTAs resident,
   blocked in griddepcontrol.wait, for every remaining stage, holding SMs
   that #989 leaves to the main stream (8 CTAs, 2 of 20 GB10 SMs) during
   SP prefill overlap.

Correctness of the trigger position: a dependent's griddepcontrol.wait
returns only after the whole primary grid has completed and its memory
operations are visible, so out (every stage), counters[0] (roce_tail) and
the send slots are final before any dependent that waits reads them; the
position changes only when dependents launch. Dependents launch only after
every CTA of the collective has executed the trigger or exited, so all of
its CTAs are already resident and the doorbell/arrival protocol, which
needs the whole grid, cannot be starved by them. The 2-slot ping-pong
argument of #989 is unchanged: within a launch stage s+1 stages into
send[(epoch+s+1)&1] only after roce_wait_peers(epoch+s); across launches
the next collective reads counters[0] and stages its send slot only after
its own pdl_sync, that is after this grid has completed, the same order as
without PDL.

Quality implications:
Launch timing only: bit-identical reductions, gathers and exchanges, wire
ABI 10 unchanged. Consumers that read a collective's output must do so
after their own griddepcontrol.wait (the deployment's PDL source audit
requires it of chain kernels).

Tests:
- python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -v (99 passed, including the
  ASan/UBSan proxy simulator and test_tilelang_kernels_emit_pdl_sync_and_trigger,
  which also checks the multi-stage kernels trigger once, under the
  last-stage guard, after the doorbell).
- Single GB10, spark-ds41f:candidate-54 image (TileLang 0.1.15): all four
  kernels (world 1, 2, 4, ring4, traced all-reduce) pass vLLM's
  deepseek_v41_pdl_sources.violations with the collectives on CHAIN; a
  captured slow PDL producer -> collective -> PDL consumer graph has 2/2
  programmatic edges, and 20 replays per case (world 1 with 1 and 4
  stages, world 2 with peer slots and flags pre-filled, 1 and 2 stages)
  with every buffer poisoned give exact outputs and advance the epoch by
  num_stages. A consumer that loads before its wait sees exactly the last
  stage's output stale, which places the trigger after the last stage's
  doorbell and before its data phase.
- Multi-rank RoCE suite (tests/gpu/test_oneshot_gpu.py): not run.

Upstream status: local candidate patch for dgx-spark-networking; replaces
the Part A half of dgx-spark-networking#6 on top of #7.
